{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: exact S4 head 6352d355bc7fd9642a75fbccf67a99597ef64067 on builder/s4-enrol-role-fixture-20261112, all 54 artifacts, for request 9a053402 under promise abbd5eba and review request f6c0add1. Manifest SHA-256 0127117b742a097f7e07f276763e7de1f608cbb76b5d0a7d71e4e056f46cb467. The head closes findings 3.2 to 3.5 and both hosted items of 1.7, and it closes 3.1 for `connections`. One defect blocks it: the new control windows count keys that any caller can mint, so one outsider can refuse hosted control, `retire` included, to every room on the Worker.\n\nRequired change 1: the control window must not fill with minted keys.\n\n- `McpRegistry.admitControl` (`packages/scope/src/worker-mcp.ts:196-208`) admits every control route through one object, `OAUTH_CLIENT_REGISTRY`, for all rooms. It counts by `r.actor`. `verifyControl` proves only that the actor's own key signed the request, and anyone can make a new key pair for each call.\n- For the routes under `/mcp/agents/\u003cid\u003e/` (bearer, grant, connections, revoke-grant and retire), the Worker skips the known-membership check (`worker-mcp.ts:96`). It then reaches that admission for any well-formed agent ID. No address window runs before `mcpRoute` (`worker.ts:805`).\n- `McpRequestWindows.admit` (`request-window.ts:25-31`) holds 4,096 identities per purpose. After that, every new identity shares one overflow counter of 120 calls a minute.\n- Trace: a sender makes about 4,216 calls a minute for each purpose, each signed by a new key with a valid `notAfter`, from one address. That fills `control` and `cleanup`. A member whose key has made no call in the last minute then gets 429 on `create`, `grant`, `bearer` and `retire` in every room. The 1 MiB retire reserve does not help, because the window refuses first. Before this head, nothing shared existed to fill.\n- The smallest fix follows S1: charge an address window (`addressKey` of `cf-connecting-ip`) before the key window at the Worker, so one address opens a bounded number of key identities. Counting only after the per-room standing check also works. Give `retire` capacity that a stranger cannot spend.\n- Witness: from one address, more fresh keys than the address bound allows, then a member's fresh key still gets its answer from `create` and from `retire`. The witness must fail at 7177b87b or at this head.\n\nRequired change 2: `docs/hosted-mcp.md:82` and `:309-310` describe \"the S4 source checkpoint\" and say that its witnesses and validation \"remain owed\". Both sentences are wrong once the head lands. State the rule, and keep the open host observation in the note.\n\nWhat I read:\n\n- 3.1. Registry and Agent `connections` (`hosted.ts:678-703`, `1208-1210`) insert no control row. Control and cleanup have separate windows, and a backward clock refuses without resetting counts (`request-window.ts:20`). A residual remains: each `grant` and `grantExisting` still charges `CONTROL_ROW_BYTES` (4 KiB) for good, because `expire` credits only the answer body and no code deletes a control row (`hosted.ts:444-451`, `551-556`). A member who controls one Agent can spend the room's 15 MiB in about 3,840 grants, about 32 minutes at the window rate, and `create` and `grant` then answer 507 for good. Request 9a053402 names `connections` as the remedy, so I do not hold the head on this. The planner decides whether grant rows need release after `notAfter`, and the docs should state the limit.\n- 3.2. `GET /oauth/authorize/context` spends an address window (`/64` for IPv6) before client resolution (`oauth-routes.ts:47-48`). An invalid 200 document enters a 60-second negative cache keyed by URL and the copied redirect policy. Non-200 replies, timeouts and network failures stay out of it (`oauth-clients.ts:130-185`).\n- 3.3. Creation, bearer rotation, code exchange, refresh and access each read fresh Agent and controller facts and refuse when the Agent's actions are not a subset (`hosted.ts:628-635`, `1639`, `1683`, `1852`, `1890-1897`; `oauth-issuer.ts:280`, `309`; `oauth-access.ts:165`). `agentRoleDecision` never reads the actor, so admin standing waives nothing. A failure after commit withholds plaintext and keeps a secret-free `codeShown` receipt. I found no path that shows a secret twice.\n- Association preflight. Only the exact `{agent, status}` absence selects pre-enrollment (`hosted.ts:248-249`). Throws, timeouts and malformed answers become 503. Row adoption requires the same actor through `fingerprintOf`, stops after two adoptions, and requires the same agent and `notAfter` (`hosted.ts:133`, `729-733`).\n- 3.4. `actTool` and every generic tool with `readOnlyHint: false` declare `_meta.ui.visibility: [\"model\"]`. `toolsForHost` keeps that field when it strips the rest of `ui`. `view_act` and declared input tools stay app-callable under ruling ec9ba020. The note cites the Visibility section at commit 298e884e.\n- 3.5. A definitive refusal deletes the pending mapping (`hosted.ts:673`). `credential` puts the secret in text content, and `structuredContent` holds only `remote`, `ends` and `entry` (`service.ts`).\n- 1.7. The Worker checks the service and the signature before the index check and `registerKnown` (`worker-mcp.ts:94-104`). It checks the body digest and the window before it resolves an OAuth client (`:114-126`). A self-signed key still passes the signature check, so in register mode a stranger can still make `registerKnown` run. All live Workers run enforce since S2, so this is a note.\n\nWhat I checked:\n\n- Scope. Main 221e11c3 is an ancestor, and the pushed branch equals the head. The builder's worktree is clean. Against main, the head changes exactly the 54 paths that the 54 artifacts cover, all at the head. The primary artifact is neither stale nor retired.\n- Cheap checks. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings. The commit-message check passes: 28 checked, 0 flagged. `git diff --check` is clean.\n- The failed gate and its follow-ups, under `docs/evidence-filing.md`. The gate at 12c24841 exited 1 in the node stage, with three failures, all in `apps/views/test/s4.test.mjs`. The follow-up chain meets the conditions:\n  - 7c0fcc3b changes three constructor arguments in that file, which no other file imports.\n  - 3b22c08f changes only `docs/mcp.md` and the generated `docs/mcp-tools.json`, after the inventory test that reads them failed.\n  - fe6fa9e7 changes only the credential assertions in `recipes.scope.test.ts`, which now assert the text placement that 3.5 requires.\n  - Together, the follow-ups ran the repaired files, a compiler, and the counting, plain and native stages. Native is the last stage.\n  - From fe6fa9e7 to the head, only the note and the manifest change. The failed runs keep exit 1, and the note claims no gate pass.\n  - I accept the chain as evidence for source fe6fa9e7.\n- Baseline tests. All eight fail at 7177b87b at their named S4 assertions, by the reports in the evidence directory. The association test distinguishes at 12c24841, with 403 expected and 201 under the fault. The earlier test that did not distinguish at 60283b41 is recorded as such.\n- Evidence. All 150 files that the manifest lists match their SHA-256 values in `/Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence`. The correspondence receipt hashes to `762a6bba…6c12` and records a difference of two paths.\n- CI, under ruling 3a5c4448. GitHub reports Page run 43274401664 and Card run 43274401667 as push events, attempt 1, completed and successful, at 77ac8113.\n- I ran no tests. One reader went through `hosted.ts` and the OAuth files independently. I confirmed in the code each point that I cite above.\n\nArchitecture: sound apart from the shared window. Admission stays in the Worker and the hosted objects. Fresh role facts come from one synchronous membership read (`hostedRoleStandingAt`), and visibility adds no server-side authority. One fixed object counts control calls for every room. That design makes the window a cross-room resource, and required change 1 has to bound it.\n\nSecurity: required change 1 is a new availability defect, not a trust gap. No secret leaks and no authority widens. Two notes for a later head:\n\n1. `credential` and `diff` carry `readOnlyHint: true` and no visibility, so a trusted card script can still call them. `credential` consumes the one-time read secret, and the app receives it. Model-only visibility on both would match 3.4.\n2. The grant-row residual under 3.1 above.\n\nSimplification: no required change. `#admitControl` and the clock helpers appear twice in `hosted.ts` (`488-491` and `997-1000`, `594` and `1003`). Three `#withheld*` functions repeat one shape. The 5-second race around a role read appears about seven times. `#existingGrantAuthority` takes five parameters. The Worker and `McpRegistry.admitControl` both verify the signature. One shared helper would cut each of these.\n\nThis verdict authorizes no landing, deployment or MCP enablement.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9bb37720899ead336b3263f3f5a44d310182513f","binding":"assigned","head":"6352d355bc7fd9642a75fbccf67a99597ef64067","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:159f33f279576c482b42126b390bc8d891695d40\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:abbd5eba6806777f902556d91b09d0566e107175\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a8c4c7dd3f4bd77d3055e87f996d83b06c787e06","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}